/* Dystoria data file (the How-To guide), moved out of index.html at v.930. Immutable: a change ships under a new name. */
window.__DYST_GUIDE = [{"t": "Welcome", "md": "*A guide to every part of Dystoria: what each piece is, what it does, and how to use it. Type a question above and the list narrows as you type, or press **Ask ✦** for a plain answer in a sentence or two. **Read the full guide** opens everything. Beside most sections a **→** takes you straight to that part of the app.*\n\n---"}, {"t": "1. What Dystoria is", "md": "Dystoria is a tool for organizing and writing **complex stories**. It holds the whole of a story in one place: the people, places, things, ideas, events and stakes, how they are connected, how they change, and the plot they move through. When you write, everything you have decided is at hand, and when you revise, your own writing is reflected back at you.\n\nThe five **modes** are the round medallions at the top left of the header:\n\n- **Plan**: the planning phase, in four tabs: **Elements** (goals, narrative layers and every story element), **Connections** (how elements relate, and the groups they form), **Places** (scenes and maps) and **Plot** (the grid of sections against threads and arcs).\n- **Write**: a clean, full-screen writing room for one section at a time.\n- **Revise**: rework what you have written, with **lenses** that read your prose back to you (rhythm, diction, pacing, tone, tension, craft) and **checks** for gaps, continuity and point of view.\n- **Review**: read the story straight through, share it with **beta readers**, and export the **manuscript**, the **book** or a **Record of Work**.\n- **Wiki** is your **story bible**: one context-rich document built from everything you have planned and written.\n\nThe story's title sits in the middle of the header; click it to rename the story. On the right: **Full screen**, **?** (this guide), the **Library**, and the **☰ menu**. The **Notepad** is the tab at the bottom of the screen, in every mode.\n\nEverything you add (notes, connections, arcs, prose) is context the app's AI reads when it helps, so its help is about *your* story, never generic.\n\n---"}, {"t": "2. Getting started: your first pages", "md": "**Get Started** (on the welcome page) opens **Where would you like to begin?** with three doors:\n\n- **Start Writing**: go straight to a blank page and find the story as you go; Dystoria spots the characters and places for you when the session ends.\n- **Start Planning**: opens **How do you like to plan?** (§3).\n- **Import**: bring in a draft or your worldbuilding notes (§29).\n\nThere is no wrong door. Every one lands in the ordinary app, and you can move between them anytime. The big **×** at the top right closes the page and takes you straight into the app (opened from **+ New Project** in the Library, it takes you back to the Library).\n\n**The tour.** When you finish your first path, such as your first writing session, a small card asks **Would you like a quick tour?** **Show me around** walks through the five modes on the example story, *Alice's Adventures in Wonderland*, and gives you your own story back at the end; **I'll explore on my own** skips it. Turn the tour on or off in **Settings → Appearance → Guidance**.\n\n**The example.** *Alice's Adventures in Wonderland* waits in your Library as a worked example. Open it to see a planned, written story in every mode.\n\n**The labyrinth.** Signed in, the Dystoria logo (at the top of the ☰ menu) opens **the labyrinth of your story**, a word-maze grown from your own elements and prose. Click anywhere to go back to your story.\n\n**Some doors need AI.** If a door runs on AI and you are signed out, a card offers **Create free account** or **I already have an account** right there, and carries on with the door you pressed once you are in.\n\n---"}, {"t": "3. How to plan: the planning doors", "md": "**Start Planning** on the first page, or the **compass** icon beside Plan's tabs (**How to plan**), asks **How do you like to plan?** Three ways in:\n\n- **Guided** is a full-screen planner that goes step by step: **Goals → Structure → Elements → Review**. Set what the story is for, choose its narrative layers, roll or add a first cast, and review the lot.\n- **Explore** finds the story in conversation: a full-screen Q&A that asks about your idea and files each answer into your notes, turning what you say into elements.\n- **Premise**: start from your own few lines. Give it a working title (optional) and a few lines of your idea, press **Turn it into a starting point →**, and Dystoria reads them like notes: it proposes the characters, places and other elements they describe for you to review and approve.\n\nEvery door lands in an ordinary Plan tab, and **←** (top left) or **Esc** steps back a page. **I'll look around on my own** skips the doors and opens Plan.\n\n---"}, {"t": "4. Plan → Elements: goals, layers and story elements", "md": "**Elements** is the list view of everything in the story, top to bottom:\n\n- **Goals** are the kind of story, its feel, who it's for. **+ goal** sits beside the title. Goals bias what the app rolls and ground every review, prompt and wiki pass. They are never inferred; they are yours.\n- **Narrative Layers**: **Genre · Context · Theme · Plot · POV**. **+ layer** (beside the title) lists them; click a layer's label to open its card. The **Plot** row holds your **threads and arcs** as pills, with one **+** that asks **Thread** or **Arc** and opens the same builders the Plot grid uses (§12, §14). Clicking the **Plot** label opens the **Plot card**: your threads (with **Organize: role, type & health**), your arcs, the story's overall shape and its expected beats, and your notes.\n- **Story Elements**: **Characters, Groups, Places, Artifacts, Concepts, Events, Stakes**. **+ element** (beside the title) lists every kind with its count; each filled row ends with its own small **+**.\n\n**Only what you have added shows.** A layer or category appears once it holds something. The **+ layer** and **+ element** menus reach the rest.\n\n**Pills.** Click an element's pill to open its **card** (§6). A star or other mark before a character's name shows a prominent story role; an **eye** marks a viewpoint character.\n\n**Filtering.** The **Filter** pill and **search** box at the right of Plan's bar show one part of the page (goals, narrative layers, or one kind of element) or find an element by name. Under each category's name, small **filter pills** narrow that row: places, artifacts and the rest by **type** (**All types**); characters by **role** (**All roles**) and by **type** (**All types**: human, elf, machine…), and the two combine (*Protagonist* + *Human* shows the human protagonists). A filter only appears once there are two or more different values to choose between.\n\n**Your categories.** The **✎** beside *Story Elements* opens **Categories**: rename, recolour, reorder, show or hide each kind, for this story or all your stories, and **make a category of your own**, built on one of the standard kinds.\n\n**Two ways to add.** **Add** names exactly what you want. **Roll** draws from a word pool, biased by your genre and goals, for surprise; a **🎲** forges a name in the style of the kind of thing it is.\n\n---"}, {"t": "5. Characters & the element builders", "md": "Every kind of element has a **builder**, the card where it is made and edited. It opens from **+** on a row, from **＋ Element**, and from **✎ Edit** on an element's card.\n\n**The character builder** has two tabs:\n\n- **Characteristics** are the dimensions: **Sex, Age, Type, Archetype, Occupation, Position** and anything you add. An empty dimension shows a **＋**; **↻** rolls it again; **⚭** beside a Type pairs it into a hybrid that reads as one line (*Human – Machine*). **＋ add a dimension** lists the standard ones and **Your dimensions**; **＋ new dimension…** makes your own, and **✦ Suggest its values** can fill it for you.\n- **Drive** is what moves them: **motivations**, **obstacles** and **stakes**, as many of each as the character has. The pickers offer anything in the story, or a new word.\n\n**Generate** at the foot rolls a whole character: **Sketch**, **Fuller** or **Rich**. **✦ Break it down** turns a line of your own prose (*a weary blacksmith trying to buy back his daughter's indenture before the winter fair*) into traits **and** Drive at once, adding to what is there, so it is safe to press twice.\n\n**Families and kinds.** A character's **Type**, and the **Kind** of a place, artifact, event or concept, comes from families of words. **＋ New family** makes your own; **✦ Suggest its kinds** proposes 8, 12 or 20 for you to tick (for this **Story**, or **My pool** for every story), and **✦ Suggest more for …** extends any family.\n\n**The other builders** have the same shape with the dimensions that fit: a place's terrain, climate and atmosphere; an artifact's material and purpose; a group's kind and **Who or what is in it**; an event's **What changes** (before → after) and **Because of**; a stake's **Who could lose it** and **Threat** (§7).\n\n**Your pool.** Words and dimensions you add can stay with this story or go into **My Dystoria pool** (Settings → Data), which follows your account to every story and device.\n\n---"}, {"t": "6. The element card", "md": "Clicking any element (a pill in Elements, a row in a drawer, a name in Review or the Wiki) opens its **card**, the roll-up of everything about it.\n\n- **The name** is the title. Click it to rename it everywhere. **+ alias** under it adds other names it goes by; every alias is recognised in your prose and the wiki.\n- **The summary**: one sentence, with **✦ Enrich** to draft or deepen it from your material.\n- **Characteristics**: a table, one row per dimension, with **✎ Edit** on the title line to open the builder.\n- **Drives**, **Connections**, **Membership** (the groups it belongs to), **Arcs**, and a character's speech and thought lines by scene. Each section has a small **✦ Enrich** pill on its title line.\n- **Notes**: your notes about it. **✦ Enrich** turns rough notes into a clean, formatted copy; a **Rough notes** pill switches back to what you wrote (still kept in the Notepad).\n- **The footer** holds icons that show their names on hover: the **map mark** (open it where it stands on the map), **✦ Explore** (a Q&A about it), the **quill** (write about it), the **notepad**, and the **trash** on the right to delete it.\n\n**How it looks.** The portrait's **✎** adds or replaces its image, or **◇ Change map icon…**. Humans are drawn as figures from their name and sex.\n\n---"}, {"t": "7. Stakes & conflict", "md": "A **stake** is a story element: something that could be **lost**. The **Stakes Builder** (**+ stake**) asks for its name, **Who could lose it** (people or groups, several if need be), its **Threat** (who or what could take it), and its **Kind of conflict**. **✦ Break it down** fills it from a line of prose. A **Still open:** line names what is missing.\n\n**The Stakes card** (click the **Stakes** label in Elements) is where the story's conflict lives:\n\n- **The story chiefly runs against** four lanes: *another · themselves · society · the world* (and *name it more exactly*), with a row for each Part if the story has them.\n- **The story's own stake**, pinned at the top.\n- Every stake, **grouped by** *Kind of conflict*, *Who could lose it* or *Threat*, with who could lose it ⟵ its threats, and a strip of the sections where it is in play.\n- Under each stake, **Wants** and **In the way** for each character who could lose it. **＋** writes straight into that character's Drive.\n\nConflict is read from your stakes; nothing is graded. In **Places**, the **Scene Context** drawer lists the stakes in play in each section (§11).\n\n---"}, {"t": "8. Events: what changed, and why", "md": "An **event** is something that happens. Its builder holds **What changes** (**Before** → **After**) and **Because of**: **＋ an earlier event**, with the verb switching between *caused by* and *made possible by*. Its card shows **Because of** and **Leads to**.\n\nIn **Places**, the **Scene Context** drawer lists the events of each section with **+ event**, and two tools:\n\n- **‹ sweep ›** asks one question, **What changed?**, across every event in book order (‹ › or the arrow keys step through; edits save as you type).\n- **The domino walk** (the domino icon on a selected event) asks *\"…happened because…?\"* and builds the chain backwards, event by event.\n\n**Gap & Chekhov** (Revise → Checks) flags a cause that comes after its effect, or events that cause each other in a circle.\n\n---"}, {"t": "9. Connections & groups: how elements relate", "md": "**Connections** (the second Plan tab) is where the relationships of a story live. A **connection** between two elements reads as a sentence (*Ora is Bram's sister*; *The Council owes the Guild*) and can carry more than one dimension. A connection holds for the whole story, and reaches the rest of the app: Places, the Notepad, the Wiki, and every AI read.\n\n**The Story Legend** (the drawer that opens first) lists every element by kind, with a search and each one's number of connections.\n\n- **Press an element** to put it **in the middle**, with everything it is connected to around it.\n- **⇢** on another row connects it to the one in the middle; **＋ Element** brings a new one in.\n- **◎** on a selected pill puts that one in the middle; **Back to groups** returns.\n\n**Groups** (the drawer's second tab) are a name and the elements in them, arranged by a structure: a family, a faction, a chain of command, a school of thought, a region and what lies inside it, a chain of cause and effect. **＋ Group** opens the **Group builder**. Choose its kind (under *Beings, Places, Things, Ideas, Events, Mixed*, or **＋ New family** for your own), name it, and add **Who or what is in it**. A group can itself become an element with its own connections. Its kinds sit under folding headings in the drawer.\n\n- **The lens** (the dropdown on a group) changes how the same group is read. A family can be a tree of descent or who lives under one roof; **Battle lines** shows only who is set against whom.\n- **Arranging.** Drag elements where you want them; **Auto-arrange** lays the group out afresh; **Save arrangement** keeps yours, and **Restore saved** puts it back.\n- **A selected pill** shows **link** (drag to connect), **shape and colour** (how it looks here), **◎** and **×**. A connection's card sets **How the line looks** (solid, dotted, dashes) and its colour, either its feeling's or your own.\n- **Text** (the drawer's third tab) adds text boxes to a group's picture.\n\n**Every kind of element has a shape**, so you can tell them apart at any size, even on the e-ink theme: a character is a rounded pill, a place a clipped plaque, an artifact a chamfered stone, a group a shield, an event a chevron, a concept a diamond, a stake a pennant.\n\n---"}, {"t": "10. Places: scenes, maps & zones", "md": "**Places** (the third Plan tab) shows your elements laid out in space. The toggle at the top centre switches between:\n\n- **Scenes**: a board for every scene of every section. The navigator (**‹ ◇ 1 Title ▾ ›**) steps through sections and their scenes, or opens the list. A new scene starts empty, with a banner offering to carry elements over. The large **place title** names the scene's setting; click it to choose or create one.\n- **Maps** are maps of your world, independent of the sections: the picker (**◇ NAME ▾**) makes, renames and deletes maps, and **◈ Put a map on this one…** nests a map inside another (a city on a world); its marker takes you inside.\n\n**＋ Element** adds a story element here; **✦ Explore** opens a Q&A about the elements on this board; **⋯** holds **⇣ Bring from a map…**, **⇡ Put this scene's places on a map…** and **Clear map**.\n\n**The left drawers:**\n\n- **Story Legend** (**Map Legend** on a map) lists every element by kind: **✓ In scene** or **＋ Add**. Press a placed one to find it.\n- **Zones** are the drawing tools: **boundary**, **path**, **shapes** and **grid** (with **Scale** or **Freeform** spacing). Select a drawing to name, recolour, move or delete it. A closed drawing with a label becomes a **zone**, such as a Place's extent or *Off-stage, At stake, Remembered, Known…*, and knows what lies inside it. An *At stake* zone holding someone with no stake offers **⚑ name their stake**.\n- **Images** and **Text**: pictures and text boxes on the board.\n\n**Selecting an element** clears the way: the tabs step aside and the **Scene Context** drawer on the right shows only what it is connected to. **Double-click** a name to rename it. A selected character can **say** or **think**, in speech and thought bubbles that belong to the scene.\n\n---"}, {"t": "11. The Scene Context drawer", "md": "The drawer on the right of **Places** (**Scene Context** on a scene, **Map Context** on a map) holds the elements that don't stand on the board: **Concepts · Groups · Events · Stakes**, each with a count and its own **+** (**+ concept**, **+ group**, **+ event**, **+ stake**).\n\n- **Select a row** to see its connections drawn onto the board; its buttons open the element's **notepad**, its **card**, its **wiki** entry, or remove it.\n- **Drag from a row's circle** (or its port) onto an element on the board to connect them.\n- **A group appears by itself** when two or more of its members stand on the board: *The Cell · 3 of 5 here*.\n- **Stakes** lists the stakes in play here: the story-wide ones in every section, a section's own only in its own.\n\n---"}, {"t": "12. The Plot grid: sections, threads & cards", "md": "**Plot** (the fourth Plan tab) is a grid: **rows are your sections**, **columns are threads and arcs**. Its corner reads *Plot*.\n\n- **Threads** are storylines, **arcs** are how one element changes (§14). **+** at the head of the columns adds either; a new thread opens its card first to be named and described. All threads are equal. To rank them, set *Main plot* or *Subplot* in the **Thread organizer** (from the Plot card in Elements).\n- **Cards.** Every cell holds a card. Its head shows the **POV** (the eye), the **Event** and the **Stakes** in force there; open it for its notes (the **Notes · POV** tab), its elements (**In scene**, **+ add element**), **✦ Summarize**, **Write** (a writing session with the card's context), **✦ Where next** (§13) and **✦ Explore**. A closed card with several scenes shows a tab for each.\n- **Sections.** Drag a section's **diamond** to reorder it. The **＋** between sections adds a section, a **Part** (a group of sections), or a prologue / epilogue. **start** sets the story's opening date, and the pill above each diamond the time since the previous section.\n- **The bar.** Undo and redo; **– 3 COLS +** for fewer or more columns; **Filter** narrows the grid to the cards that hold the elements you tick (they combine); **Export ▾** holds **Show filters**, **Compare arcs** and **Export .xls**.\n- **The column head** opens the thread's card (its point of view, key elements, stakes, the concepts driving it, its shape) or the arc's card.\n\n---"}, {"t": "13. Where next: planning a path through a section", "md": "**✦ Where next** (on every Plot card, beside Write and Explore) plans a section step by step. It offers three possible next steps, each labelled with its kind: *feel · want · block · try · turn · land*. Take one and three more grow beneath it; the two you passed stay as faint ghosts you can come back to.\n\n- **×** drops an offer; **↻ three more** asks again; **≈ close** asks for *something close to this*; **← step back** undoes a step; **end the section here** lands it. Type your own step whenever you like, no AI needed.\n- **Guiding thought** (a line at the top) steers every step; **+ avoid** says what not to do; **+ the book** is a standing instruction for the whole story. **Lenses** (*made of* interiority, action, dialogue, environment, sensory, relationship, stakes) shape the steps.\n- **⧉ Copy to card note** writes the path into the card; **✦ Re-seed from the page** ticks off steps your prose already covers.\n\nIn **Write**, the **Plan** drawer brings a section's steps into the room: **⌘⇧↓** marks the current step written and shows the next.\n\n---"}, {"t": "14. Arcs: who changes, and how", "md": "An **arc** tracks how an element, usually a character, **changes** across the story. Add one from the Plot row in Elements (**+ → Arc**), from the Plot card, or as a column in the Plot grid, and its **arc card** opens:\n\n- **Who changes?** and **what changes**: each aspect **From → To** (*isolated → belonging*); **＋ change** adds another.\n- **Driven by** is what pushes the change; **Cost / stakes** is what it costs.\n- **Beats**: optional. **Load a structure ▾** offers the four character-arc shapes (*positive change, fall, flat, disillusionment*) and the classic story structures as goalposts (*Three-Act, Save the Cat, Tragedy, The Quest, Overcoming the Monster, Voyage and Return, Rags to Riches, Rebirth, Mystery, Kishōtenketsu*), or **＋ beat** adds your own; each can be pinned to a section.\n- **Momentum**: how far the arc has actually moved on the page, read from your prose.\n\nAn arc's column in the grid holds the cards where the change happens; the element's card lists its arcs. **Compare arcs** (Plot → Export ▾) sets them side by side.\n\n---"}, {"t": "15. Point of view: whose eyes", "md": "Set the book's **POV** in Elements (the **POV** layer): first, second or third person, limited or omniscient, and the **narrator**. Characters the reader sees through wear an **eye**, before their name in Elements and above their figure in Places.\n\n- A **Plot card's** eye chooses whose eyes a section or a single scene is told through; a viewpoint that departs from the book's shows in gold.\n- A **thread** can have eyes of its own once the book is told through more than one.\n- **Perspective check** (Revise → Checks, no AI needed) pins a note wherever the prose breaks the point of view: first person in a third-person book, a first-person section that never says *I*, a narrator reporting another character's thoughts.\n- Where next, Explore and the craft review keep to what the viewpoint character can know.\n\n---"}, {"t": "16. The Notepad: notes on everything", "md": "The **Notepad** is the tab at the bottom of the screen, in every mode (**×** closes it). It is where the depth of a story lives, and everything in it is context for the Wiki and the AI.\n\n- **The head bar**: **ALL ▾** (show one kind of note: goals, plot, threads, sessions, a layer, a category), a **search**, **✦ Research** (§23) and **Export ▾**.\n- **The shelf**: a pill for every element, section, thread, arc, goal, group and writing session. **General Notes** is the scratchpad for anything that belongs nowhere yet.\n- **The card** (left, when an element is selected) shows its name, its **story role** (with **Change →**), and its pills grouped by category. Click a pill to write notes about *that* aspect. Each section's title has a shortcut to where it is edited: the builder, the **Drive** tab, the Plot grid, or **Connections**. **Open card** opens its full card (§6).\n- **The pad** (right) holds the notes themselves, with a format bar: **mic** (dictate), **H** heading, **B**, **I**, **•** bullets, **☑** checklists, **⊞** tables, **🖼** images, **🔗** links.\n\nSessions, Explore conversations, craft reviews and research file themselves here too, each under its own pill. The Notepad is also the record of how the story was made.\n\n---"}, {"t": "17. Write mode: the writing room", "md": "**Write** is a clean, full-screen room for one section at a time. When you enter, a card asks where the writing goes: **Continue here**, **Continue another section ▾**, or **Start a new section**.\n\n**The drawers** open from tabs at the edges and stay out of the way:\n\n- **Left: Focus · Questions · Plan.** **Focus** sets what the session is about: a **focus element**, a **prompt**, a writing **guide** or **exercise**, a **word goal**, a **timer**. **Questions** keeps the questions you pin (⌘⌥Q, below). **Plan** brings the steps planned for this section into the room (§13).\n- **Right: Page · Keys · Tidy-ups.** **Page** sets **Charcoal** or **Paper**, the view's size and the ink. **Keys** lists every key, below. **Tidy-ups** can **Tidy up** what you just wrote, and *Fix as I write* (capitalization, obvious typos, punctuation, names).\n\nThe column's top bar shows your focus elements, the prompt and the section's title (click it to rename the section). Beside the page are **Dictate** (or hold **⌘** alone) and **Music**, a focus soundscape.\n\n**Ending a session.** The card reads **Adding to Section N ▾**. **Keep** adds your writing there (*or make it a new scene*), **Discard** drops it. Then **Review Session Elements** offers the characters, places and other elements your prose mentions that the story doesn't have yet: **Accept all**, or pick. If you planned steps, it asks whether to carry the rest to the next session.\n\n---"}, {"t": "The writing keys", "md": "In the writing room these keys are live, and every one is a row in the **Keys** drawer (the zoom and ink ones are in the **Page** drawer). ⌘ on a Mac, Ctrl elsewhere.\n\n- **⌘B / ⌘I / ⌘U**: bold, italic, underline; **⌘⇧X** strikes through; **⌘\\** clears formatting. With nothing selected they act on the word you clicked.\n- **“ ( [ {** open a pair and put the caret between them; with text selected they wrap it.\n- **⌘⌥Q asks three questions** about what you have just written: the selection, the paragraph or the session. Questions only, never a rewrite. Pin the ones worth keeping; they wait in the **Questions** drawer, and **⇢** sends one to Revise.\n- **A click lights the word** under it without selecting it; **double-click** takes the sentence, **triple-click** the paragraph.\n- **⌘⇧⏎** starts a **new scene** (the ◇ mark), splitting the line at the caret.\n- **⌘⇧↓** marks the current plan step written and shows the next.\n- **Hold ⌘** on its own for a moment to dictate; let go to stop.\n- **⌘+ / ⌘−** zoom the view, **⌘0** resets it; **⌘< / ⌘>** lighten and darken the ink, **⌘⌥< / ⌘⌥>** change its colour.\n\n---"}, {"t": "18. Revise mode: lenses & checks", "md": "**Revise** is where you rework what you have written. Its bar: **Suggest | Edit** (suggest changes for approval, or edit directly), **Images** (a book cover, or an image at the cursor), the view's size, **Find & replace** (⌘F, whole words by default), **Version history** (snapshots per section to browse, compare and restore), **Notes ▾** (your notes, reader comments, AI feedback and the Notepad down the margin), and the section navigator (**◇ N Title ▾**). Section titles and Part names are editable in place; click a **section divider** to choose its symbol.\n\nFour drawers open from the tabs at the edges:\n\n- **Organize**: **＋ Section**, **＋ Part**, **＋ Scene** (a break at your cursor), **Split at cursor**, and drag to reorder.\n- **Patterns** are the lenses: **Hemingway style, Rhythm & Sound, Emotional weather, Style fingerprint, Sentence architecture, Diction, Pacing, Tone, Rhetoric, Reach, Tension: questions & resolution**, and **Craft review** (§19). Each paints a reading onto your prose.\n- **Checks**: **Perspective check** (no AI), and three ✦ passes: **Gap & Chekhov** (a stake never paid off, a character with no connections, a place never entered, an object that vanishes, causes out of order), **Continuity pass** (a trait that changed, a destroyed place reappearing, a timeline slip), **Grammar & prose review**. A pass shows its progress full-screen, and **Keep working** carries on while it runs.\n- **Research**: §23.\n\n**Select text** for the selection bar: **Refract** (a passage held up to you as concepts, words and angles, never replacement prose), and **Define** (the dictionary sense beside what the word means *in your story*; your gloss feeds the Wiki's Glossary).\n\n**Reader feedback**: the gold count on the Revise medallion or on **Share** opens every reader comment: **Reply**, **Resolve**, **Go to passage**.\n\nNone of the lenses rewrites anything. They read your writing back to you so you can refine your voice, your style and the story yourself.\n\n---"}, {"t": "19. The craft review & the craft-notes loop", "md": "**Craft review** (at the foot of Patterns) is an honest AI read across pace, scene and description, characterisation, plot clarity, and prose and voice, with strengths and weaknesses for each.\n\nEach aspect has a **+ Notepad** button that saves that piece of feedback into your Notepad, dated and labelled. Because it lives there, later craft reviews can see it and tell you whether you have **addressed it, partly addressed it, or left it open**, so the review becomes a running conversation about your craft. The **Style Challenge** (from your Style fingerprint) goes further: measured habits of your prose, with the evidence before the dare.\n\n---"}, {"t": "20. Review mode: reading, beta readers & export", "md": "**Review** reads the story straight through. Its bar: **Suggestions | Original** (with suggested edits, or as written), **Share**, **Export ▾**, find (⌘F), and the section navigator. The **View** drawer switches between the **Manuscript** and the **Book** (a formatted book with a title page, chapters and front and back matter).\n\n- **Share** makes a **reader link**. Anyone with it can read and leave **comments anchored to passages**, no account needed; a click takes a word, twice a sentence, three times a paragraph. Readers can **mark the passages that land**. Every comment comes back to you (Revise → reader feedback), and you can reply. Turn the link off any time. **Send to your beta readers…** sends it to the readers you have (§25).\n- **Export ▾**: **PDF**, **Word**, **ePub**, **Part of the story…** (one Part, one thread, or everything one character does), and **Record of Work…** (§21).\n\nClick any element's name in the text to open its card.\n\n---"}, {"t": "21. Record of Work: proof it's yours", "md": "Dystoria keeps a private **record of how your work is made**: where each piece of text came from (**typed, dictated, pasted, imported, AI-assisted or a co-author's**), never your keystrokes or the text you paste in. Only you see it.\n\nOpen it from **Review → Export ▾ → Record of Work…** or **Settings → Data → Open your record**:\n\n- **What it's made of**, by section; each piece of **outside text**, with **Show where** (light it up in Revise), **Quote & cite**, or **I've rewritten it, check**; your sessions; a **Replay** of the writing; and the **thinking trail** of your planning.\n- **Issue a Record of Work** makes a public, printable link: the summary, or the summary and sessions, optionally with the thinking trail and a replay (without the words). Anyone can **Check a copy** against it, and it says plainly **what this Record can't show**.\n\n---"}, {"t": "22. The Wiki: your story bible", "md": "**Wiki** is the story bible as **one context-rich document**: every element with its identity, aliases, connections, arcs, where it appears and your notes; the groups; the threads and arcs; the **Connections**; a **Glossary** of your story's own words; the questions the story raises; and a reading list.\n\n- **✦ Update Wiki** reads what has changed since the last update and revises just those entries (new ones join their category). A full-screen page shows its progress, and **Keep working** carries on while it runs.\n- Two layers, switched in the **Contents** drawer: **the record**, everything computed from what you have written (always current, no AI), and **✦ Portraits**, the paragraphs the AI writes about each element, thread, arc and group.\n- **Every element name is a link** to its entry. Find (⌘F) searches it.\n- **Questions the story raises**: the open tensions in your material; often where the next draft wants to go.\n- **Suggested Reading**: real, well-regarded books that share your story's themes, questions and structures, chosen against your writer profile and goals, never one you have marked as read.\n- **Export ▾**: the wiki as a Word document or HTML.\n\n---"}, {"t": "23. Research: real sources, filed into your notes", "md": "**✦ Research** (the Notepad's head bar, and the **Research** tab in Revise) is a grounded research assistant. Ask in plain language; the answer already knows your story's facts and carries **real references**: inline `[1]` markers and a **Sources** list built only from pages actually retrieved. An answer with no live sources is marked as model memory and cannot be filed.\n\nHighlight what is worth keeping (or *add all*), point each clip at the element it belongs to, and **Import** files it under that element's **✦ Research** pill in the Notepad, with its sources. **⤢** opens the drawer full-screen. Research feeds the wiki, labelled as sourced, and never feeds story generation, so a citation can never leak into your fiction.\n\n---"}, {"t": "24. Explore & how Dystoria's AI works", "md": "**✦ Explore** (the ✦ stars in Plan, on element cards, on Plot cards, and the planning doors) opens a full-screen Q&A about whatever you are looking at. It asks, you answer (type or dictate), and **End & Organize** files the conversation into your notes and proposes the elements it found.\n\n**How the AI works.** Every AI tool sits behind the **✦** star. Whenever you use one it gathers the relevant context (your writer profile, goals, every element's notes, connections, arcs and aliases, your prose section by section, the threads and their stakes) and works only from it. Two principles hold: it **invents nothing** about your story (apart from research and the reading list it uses only your own material), and it exists to **reflect the story back to you so you can refine it**, never to write it for you.\n\n- **Settings → AI** explains each ✦ feature and has **Use AI features**. Switch it off and every star goes; planning, writing, revision, the deterministic lenses and every export keep working exactly as before.\n- **Access.** AI features need a free account. Each account has a daily allowance, shown in Settings → AI. Everything else needs no AI at all.\n\n---"}, {"t": "25. Co-writing, sharing & community", "md": "- **Co-writing.** In the Library, **Co-Author** (or **People**, once shared) invites another writer by email as an **Editor** (writes and approves changes) or a **Suggester** (suggests only). The invitation waits for them when they sign in; they **Accept**, **Decline** or choose **Later**. Stories others share with you sit under **Shared with you** in the Library.\n- **Writing together live.** A co-author's disc sits beside the Wiki medallion. Hover to see where they are, click to **Go to where they are** or **Chat**. While someone is typing in a section it is held for them (*Mara is writing here*); saves from two people merge section by section. The chat window floats and folds away; a green handset starts a voice call.\n- **Community** (the people icon in the header, when signed in) has **Friends** and **Beta readers**. Beta readers is an exchange of manuscripts (18+): set up a reader profile, **Find readers**, swap, read for others, or send a manuscript with your questions and a due date. **Invite** (and *Invite a friend* in the menu) invites a friend to Dystoria.\n- **A reader link**: §20.\n- **Feedback to the maker**: **Feedback** in the ☰ menu.\n\n---"}, {"t": "26. Your account", "md": "**Sign In** (in the ☰ menu) takes your **email or username** and password; **Create account** needs a real email, a username (how collaborators see you), and the 13+ and Terms box ticked.\n\n**Your account** shows your plan, a **photo** (**Upload a photo** replaces your initial on discs and cards), and your **Profile**: about you, your interests, favourite books and authors. It shapes how the AI reflects your work back to you and what the wiki suggests you read. Your profile and your **My Dystoria pool** are saved to your account and follow you to every device.\n\n**Signing out** clears this browser, every Dystoria tab and window, for the next person; stories saved to your account come back when you sign in. If a sign-in ends on its own (it expires, or you signed out on another device), your stories are **put away** in this browser, out of the library, and return the moment you sign in again. No other account ever sees them.\n\n---"}, {"t": "27. Your Library", "md": "The **Library** (the shelf icon at the top right) holds every story you have started. The switch beside **+ New Project** shows it as **books on shelves** (the grid) or as a **list** of cards with each story's date, word count, cover and summary.\n\n- **+ New Project** starts a new story and opens **Where would you like to begin?**; **Import** brings in a draft or notes (§28).\n- **A book** shows its cover, or its title on the cloth if it has none. Click it to open the story. Under it: **Co-author**, **Share** (a reader link), **Export** and **Delete**.\n- **Turn a book over** with the folded corner at its top right. The back holds the **summary** (write your own, or **✦ Draft** one), its kind and word count, when it was started and last saved, its shelf, and **+ Cover**.\n- **Shelves.** **+ Shelf** makes one. Click a shelf's name to rename it; its **…** moves it up or down, or deletes it (the stories on it are kept). The arrow beside the name folds a shelf away.\n- **Reorder** by dragging a book to a new place, or onto another shelf. On a touch screen, hold the book for a moment first. With a book focused, **Alt + arrow** moves it one place.\n- **Select** (or ⌘/Ctrl-click a book) picks several at once: **Add to shelf**, **Take off shelf**, or **Delete** them together. Shift-click picks a run.\n- **Shared with you** holds the stories other writers have invited you into.\n\n**Empty projects tidy themselves away.** A project with no words and nothing added to it is deleted when you open another story or start a new one, and when you come back to Dystoria.\n\nYour shelves and their order follow you to every device when you are signed in. The open story is ringed in gold. Every story autosaves as you work and syncs to the cloud when you are signed in; a very large library keeps some stories in the cloud and loads them when you open them.\n\n---"}, {"t": "28. Importing an existing story", "md": "**Import** (in the Library, and on the first page) turns prose or notes into a Dystoria story. Choose **Story prose** or **Notes & worldbuilding**, paste the text or open a `.txt`, `.md` or `.docx` file, and press **Import & Structure**. A full-screen page shows what it is reading and what it has found so far.\n\n- **What was found**: a summary, counts by kind and every name. **Approve all**, or **Review each**: every card can be renamed, retyped or dropped, and **See connections (N)** shows its relationships.\n- **Story prose** is split into **sections** only where the text has a real division: a chapter heading, a numbered part, a scene-break line. The importer finds the characters (with their sex and motivations), groups, places and artifacts (with their characteristics), events, stakes (with who could lose them and what threatens them), and the connections the text actually states, placing each element in the sections it belongs to. Title pages and author's notes go into **General Notes**, not the prose.\n- **Notes & worldbuilding** reads character sheets and world notes instead, and files each element's details into its Notepad and Wiki without touching your manuscript.\n\nEverything imported stays with that story. You land in **Plan → Elements** to carry on.\n\n---"}, {"t": "29. Settings, the menu & backups", "md": "**Settings** (the gear in the ☰ menu) has four tabs:\n\n- **Appearance**: **Ember** theme (the warm dark header) or Classic; **E-ink** (high contrast, no animation; it turns on by itself on an e-ink tablet); **Guidance** (the tour on or off); **Layout**.\n- **Data**: **My Dystoria pool** (your own words and dimensions, for every story, with **＋ Add from this story…**); **Back Up** (every story as one file), **Restore** (merges a backup with what is here) and **Restore points** (full backups taken before each update); **Record of Work** (**Open your record**); **Auto-Export** (a Markdown copy of every session to a folder of your choice).\n- **AI**: what each ✦ feature does, **Use AI features**, and your AI usage.\n- **About**: the Privacy Policy and Terms of Service.\n\n**The ☰ menu** holds **Sign In** / your account, the **Library**, **Session statistics** (your writing over time), **About Dystoria**, **Feedback**, **Support Dystoria** (pay what you want) and **Settings**. After an update, a signed-in writer sees *what's new* once, with a restore point taken first.\n\n**Getting your work out**, at three levels: the **story** from Review (PDF, Word, ePub), the **wiki** from Wiki (Word, HTML), and the whole **library** from Settings → Data → Back Up.\n\n---"}, {"t": "30. On a phone", "md": "Dystoria works on a phone, pared down to what fits:\n\n- The header keeps **?**, the **Library** and the **☰** menu; the mode medallions sit under it.\n- **Plan** is the **Elements** page; its filters fold into one **ALL** dropdown beside the search.\n- The **Notepad** is the tab at the bottom; an element's card fills the screen, and a tap on a pill turns to its notes. Tables made on a computer still show.\n- **Write** has a large gold **Dictate** pill; on an iPhone press and hold it to use the keyboard's own mic.\n- **Review** always shows the Manuscript. **Explore** and **Research** open full-screen.\n\nYour stories sync between your phone and computer when you are signed in.\n\n---"}, {"t": "31. A quick first session", "md": "1. **Get Started** → **Start Planning** → **Guided** (or **Premise**, and write a few lines about your idea).\n2. In **Elements**, set a **goal** and a couple of narrative layers; **add or roll** a few characters and a place.\n3. Open a character's **card**; give them a motivation in the builder's **Drive** tab, and a few notes.\n4. In **Connections**, put a character in the middle and connect two others to them.\n5. In **Plot**, add a second section, a **thread** with a **stake**, and an **arc** for the character who changes.\n6. **Write** the first section from your focused elements, and **Keep** it at the end.\n7. In **Revise**, open **Patterns** and read the section through one lens.\n8. Open the **Wiki** and press **✦ Update Wiki** to see your world written up.\n\n---"}, {"t": "32. Writing a *good* story: the long game", "md": "Dystoria won't write your story, and no guide can hand you talent. But the craft of a good story is less a secret than a **practice**, and the app is built around that practice.\n\n- **Stories become good by revision.** Almost no first draft is good. Stories earn their quality as the author returns, reconsiders and revises, testing whether every character has a real motivation, whether each thread carries a true stake, whether the arcs actually move, whether the connections mean something. Plan holds the whole shape; the Notepad holds the thinking; **Revise's lenses** show you your own prose from outside; the **Wiki** shows what the story adds up to; the **questions the story raises** point at what is unresolved.\n- **Learn from the writers who came before.** The wiki's **Suggested Reading** puts your story in the company of the books that share its questions, so you read *with purpose*, against your own work in progress.\n- **Let the tools double as craft prompts.** A clear goal; a want, an obstacle and a stake for every character; a stake for every thread; a From → To for every arc; a group for every allegiance; a focused set of elements for every session. Each is a question the craft asks of every story.\n\nThe good story is usually already latent in your material. The work, and the pleasure, is in returning to it with care, element by element. That returning is what Dystoria is for.\n\n---"}, {"t": "33. Common questions", "md": "**Is it free? Do I need an account?** Dystoria is free to use, with a pay-what-you-want way to support it (**Support Dystoria** in the ☰ menu). You can plan and write straight away; a free account is needed for the ✦ AI features, cloud sync, sharing and co-writing.\n\n**Is my work saved?** Yes. It is autosaved as you go, synced to your account when signed in, with **Back Up** as a file and automatic **restore points** before each update (Settings → Data).\n\n**Is my story private?** Yes, by default. Sharing is opt-in, one story at a time, and a reader link can be turned off at any time.\n\n**Someone else uses this computer. Are my stories safe?** Signing out clears this browser; your stories come back when you sign in (§26).\n\n**Do I have to use the maps, the random words, or the AI?** No. Places is one tab of Plan; **Add** takes exactly what you type; every ✦ feature is optional, and you can switch them all off in Settings → AI.\n\n**How do I change how a character looks?** On their card: the portrait's **✎** changes the image or the map icon. Their sex and name shape the drawn figure.\n\n**Where are threads?** In **Plan → Elements**, in the **Plot** row, and as columns in the **Plot** grid.\n\n**How do I delete something?** Open its card and press the **trash** at the bottom right.\n\n**Can I bring in a story I've already started?** Yes, with **Import** (§28).\n\n**Can I prove I wrote it?** Your **Record of Work** (§21).\n\n**Can friends read it and give me feedback? Can I write with someone?** Yes and yes (§20, §25).\n\n**Where do I start if it feels like a lot?** The quick first session (§31), or just add one character and open their card.\n\n**It's missing a feature I want.** Ask the **?** box; if it isn't a current feature you get a one-tap way to send it as a suggestion. **Feedback** in the menu goes straight to the maker.\n\n---"}, {"t": "Glossary", "md": "**Element** = a noun of the story: a character, group, place, artifact, concept, event or stake · **Section** = a part of the story (a chapter); a **scene** is a part of a section, and a **Part** groups sections · **Thread** = a storyline, a pill in Elements' Plot row and a column in the Plot grid · **Arc** = how one element changes, From → To, optionally in beats · **Stake** = something that could be lost, who could lose it, and what threatens it · **Connection** = a relationship between two elements, read as a sentence · **Group** = a name and the elements in it, arranged by a structure (a family, a faction, a chain of command…) · **Lens** = a way of reading; a Connections lens reads a group's structure, a Patterns lens reads your prose · **Goal** = the kind of story, its feel, who it's for · **Narrative layers** = Genre · Context · Theme · Plot · POV · **Zone** = a labelled drawing in Places that knows what lies inside it · **The record** = the wiki's computed half; **Portraits** = its AI-written half · **Record of Work** = the private record of where your text came from, and the link you can issue from it · **My Dystoria pool** = your own words and dimensions, for every story.\n\n**Drive** is what moves a character: what they **want**, the **obstacles** in the way, and what is **at stake**. A tab in the character builder, a section of their card, and part of what the AI is told about your story.\n\n---"}];
